{"kind":"artifact","text":"Two closed-environment claims now carry their qualification where they are made. Exact clean pushed head ebc0502a927ebd5c0efc59987b0dec647b639358 on request/repo-remote-link; tree f0d7072c, checkout clean, origin matching. Base: this branch's merge-base with main is c679fb73; main itself is 3a5d952c as verified at filing. Those are two values and the previous sets collapsed them into one phrase. Under planner's re-anchored round-eight child f6762079 and my promise 9d318a52.\n\nWHAT WAS UNTRUE. gitStatedEnvironment opened by calling itself the whole of the environment and saying nothing at all is inherited, then narrowed to Unix six paragraphs later. The closed-world test opened the same way and deferred its Cmd.Env and Unix limits to a later paragraph. Standing alone each opening is false, because Go's os/exec adds SYSTEMROOT to what a Windows child receives. Both now carry the qualification in the first sentence; the later paragraphs explaining why are unchanged.\n\nWHY THIS KEPT HAPPENING, since it was the sixth instance. I treated a qualification as a caveat to be discharged once per document, so it survived where I argued for the claim and vanished where I stated it. A qualification is part of the claim and travels with every restatement.\n\nTHE SWEEP. Round eight's conditions asked for the rest of internal/app and its tests, not only the two lines codex named, because a reviewer names what they opened. I grepped the package for absolute closed-environment phrasings and found no instance beyond those two.\n\nTHE PROVENANCE LINE ABOVE IS THE OTHER HALF OF THIS ROUND. Earlier sets said \"base equals current main c679fb73\". That was true when first written several heads ago and carried forward through four republications without re-deriving. Main is 3a5d952c; c679fb73 is the merge-base. The head, the tree and the branch are pinned by the commit and survive being carried; the base is a relationship between two things that both move, so it is a measurement to retake at filing rather than a line to copy.\n\nNO BEHAVIOUR CHANGES. Nothing in this head alters what any command does. The construction, the regressions, their hard controls and the citation exit-code fail-closed rule are exactly as reviewed at 5a4ee9e9.\n\nWHAT THIS ARTIFACT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT CLAIM. It does not assert that the operator's ambient environment is hostile. Planner relays at c74757f5 that hugh has reconsidered and intends that environment to be outside gitseq's threat model, and the clarified ruling has not landed. What the code does is stated here; why the project should want it is a question that ruling may reframe, and I would rather leave that open than publish a justification the threat model may not support.\n\nGATES, run by me at this exact head from a run I watched exit. go test ./... -count=1 exit 0: 34 ok and 1 with no test files, zero failures and zero cached. npm test 131 pass, 0 fail. gofmt -l . empty; go build, go vet, GOOS=windows go build ./internal/app/ and git diff --check all exit 0; make ui leaves git status empty; make ui-check exit 0. Not run by me: go test -race, and any gate on Linux — this ran on darwin/arm64 with git 2.50.1.","body":{"commit":"ebc0502a927ebd5c0efc59987b0dec647b639358","path":"internal/app"}}